
  But there's one fact I can't guess, and it determines whether phase two is even worth building — and whether phase one
  needs a server at all. A server is the expensive, hard-to-reverse decision here; a browser-local app is cheap to
  build and cheap to throw away, but it can't ever deliver a notification from another person.

  [1mThe fork:[0m who, other than you, changes these tasks?

  - [1mJust you, one device[0m — no server needed; everything lives in the browser. Cheapest by a wide margin. Rules out
    cross-person notifications entirely; the useful remainder is reminders (due dates, nudges), which is a different and
    much smaller feature than what you asked for.
  - [1mJust you, several devices[0m — needs sync, which means a server or hosted backend. "Notification" mostly means "your
    phone learns what your laptop did." Moderate cost.
  - [1mA team or shared list[0m — needs a server, accounts, and permissions. This is the case where notifications as you
    described them genuinely matter, and it's the largest scope by some distance.

  [1mMy recommendation: answer this honestly rather than aspirationally.[0m If it's really just you, I'd build the
  browser-local task app and replace "notifications" with due-date reminders — you'd get the actual value in a fraction
  of the work. Multi-user is a real project, and worth doing if that's the goal, but it shouldn't be chosen by accident.
